  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Turning warmer with a return of shower and\nthunderstorm chances for the second half of the work week.\n\nToday will provide one more day of dry weather before the\npattern turns more active for latter portions of the work week.\nCanadian high pressure remains over the Eastern Seaboard but has\ncontinued to advance closer to the Atlantic coastline. Given\nthe ridge axis is not directly bisecting the local area, some\nweak onshore gradients have allowed for light winds overnight. \nAs of 07Z/3 AM, most locations are sitting in the upper 50s to \nlow 60s. This does come with some pockets of mid 60s across \nWashington, D.C. and Baltimore, as well as near river/bay \ninfluences along and east of I-95. Skies do remain clear given \nthe presence of high pressure. A few areas of patchy fog may \ndevelop ahead of daybreak, especially across mountain valleys \nand other low-lying spots.\n\nRelative to early in the week, temperatures will return to above\naverage for mid-September. This is in response to the building\nof mid/upper heights as southern U.S. ridging flexes northward.\nAn expansive mid-level 594-dm ridge is forecast to extend from\nthe Southern Plains and Gulf Coast region up to the Mid-Atlantic\nstates. Some ensemble guidance even show the development of a\n600-dm contour over the Mid-South. These positive height\nanomalies will run around 2 to 2.5 sigma above climatology.\nAlthough there is tremendous warming aloft, this does not fully\nreflect at the surface. While conditions will be warm, high\ntemperatures should be confined to the mid/upper 80s, with a few\nlow 90s across the Allegheny mountain valleys. A shift to\nsoutherly gradients also aids in increasing humidity, but\ncertainly not oppressive by any means. The forecast does stay\ndry with subsidence aloft keeping skies mostly sunny.\n\nCanadian high pressure becomes centered just off the Atlantic\ncoast tonight. At the same time, an approaching cold front is\nexpected to briefly stall over the Ohio Valley through Thursday\nmorning/afternoon. Tonight will yield a gradual increase in\nclouds, particularly north of I-66/U.S. 50. The added clouds and\ncontinued southerly winds carries overnight lows in the low/mid\n60s. Thursday also offers the next opportunity for showers and\nthunderstorms. This largely focused west of U.S. 15, and\nespecially toward the Shenandoah Valley and points westward.\nSome erosion of this strong upper ridge will allow diurnal\nheating and terrain circulations to fire off some scattered\nstorms. As convection presses eastward, the influence of the\nstout ridge aloft should yield a weakening trend of such\nactivity. High temperatures are forecast to return to near 90\ndegrees, with the cool spot being in the mountains where mid 70s\nto low 80s will be more commonplace. Above average temperatures\ncontinue into the night with lows in the mid 60s to low 70s.\n\nThe mentioned cold front should track across the local area\nearly Friday as another dome of strong high pressure approaches\nfrom southern Ontario. Post-frontal northerly winds pick up in\nearnest with afternoon gusts to around 15 to 20 mph. It will not\nbe a completely dry forecast as showers form around the eastern\nperiphery of the retreating upper ridge. This mostly focuses \nover the central Virginia Piedmont back toward the Potomac \nHighlands. Friday presents a cooling trend with forecast highs \nin the upper 70s to mid 80s. Winds gradually turn more \nnortheasterly as high pressure builds over southern Quebec into \ninterior New England. Lows falls back into the upper 50s to mid \n60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled but uncertain pattern this weekend \ninto early next week.\n\nA high latitude trough digging across southeastern Canada should be \nenough to nudge the upper ridge back toward the south-central U.S. \nby this weekend. This allows a cold front to push further\nsouth into North Carolina by Saturday which provides a cooler\nday with less humidity. However, this reprieve is somewhat \nbrief and uncertain as additional disturbances in the atmosphere\ntrack toward the area late in the weekend, causing the front to\nwaver back and forth near the region. The overall jet pattern \nturns a bit more complex as an array of features enter the \npicture. A broad upper low retrogrades across the Gulf. At the \nsame time, a myriad of shortwaves will eject across the central \nU.S. toward the Mississippi Valley. How these impulses behave \nalong with where surface features reside will dictate any local \nweather impacts. However, all signs point toward a more active \npattern by Sunday into early next week.",
